// A priority scheduler: tasks, locks, and the run loop that decides who gets the next tick.
//
// The axis this owns is **OS territory** — the first program in the set where sysl has to behave
// like systems code rather than like an application. A run queue, a priority, a task that blocks
// and a task that wakes it are the shapes a kernel is made of, and they are made of `&T` graphs
// mutated through references: a lock points at its owner, the owner points back at the lock it
// holds, and a waiter points at what it is waiting for while that thing points at the waiter.
//
// The centrepiece is **priority inheritance**, because it is the one place where all of it has to
// work at once. A low-priority task holding a lock that a high-priority task wants is lent the
// waiter's urgency until it lets go — which means finding the holder in the ready queue and moving
// it, which is the decrease-key the heap next door exists for. Run the same three tasks with the
// lending switched off and the schedule is the classic unbounded inversion; switch it on and the
// urgent task waits for the critical section instead of for an unrelated third task. Both traces
// can be worked out on paper, and `main.sysl` asserts them character by character.
//
// What writing it turned up, in the order it bit:
//
//   * **The wait graph is a reference cycle, and `weak` is specified but not built.** A blocked
//     task points at the lock and the lock's waiter list points back at the task; an owner and the
//     lock it holds are a second cycle. `03 § weak T` names exactly this shape — "the tool for
//     back-references" — and `weak` is a reserved word the compiler otherwise knows nothing about,
//     so the program has no way to say which edge of a cycle does not own. What saves it is that a
//     scheduler takes its own graph apart: every wait ends in a wakeup and every lock is released,
//     so the last section of the checks asserts that nothing is left holding anything. That is a
//     property worth having anyway, but here it is standing in for a guarantee the language should
//     be making — a run that stops with a task still blocked leaks it, and nothing says so.
//   * **A `Buf` grows and shrinks at its end and nowhere else.** Taking a waiter out of the middle
//     of a list is `drop_one` below: compact, then pop the tail down to the new length, because
//     there is no `remove` and no `truncate` to say it in one step. Every list a scheduler keeps is
//     one that things leave from the middle of.
//   * **A task is fifteen fields with no names at the call site and no defaults.** `task` below
//     exists only to spell the five zeroes, the three `None`s and the empty list that every new
//     task starts life holding, and a reader of the constructor call cannot see which argument is
//     which. A task control block is exactly the shape that pays for this: it is mostly state that
//     starts empty and is written later by somebody else.
//   * **A nullary generic cannot be told what it is making.** `buf[&Mutex]()` is not the syntax —
//     call-site type arguments are deliberately absent (`10 § Open a`), since a type-argument list
//     and an index are the same grammar. Where the value lands somewhere already typed there is
//     nothing to do — the `buf()` filling a task's `holds` below takes its element type from the
//     field — so it is a `var` that has to say it. The reach for the other spelling is natural
//     enough that the compiler used to answer it with "the thing being called must be a name"; it
//     now names the rule and the annotation instead, which was this program's one change to the
//     compiler rather than to itself.
//   * **No enum renders itself.** `str` on one is refused — helpfully, naming the `impl Display`
//     that would answer it — and that is true of a data-carrying enum and of a plain one alike. So
//     `describe_state` below is a hand-written match from six variants to their own six names,
//     which the compiler already knows: they are the words the match arms are written with. Other
//     programs in the set write a `describe` too, but for a *message* built out of a variant's
//     payload; this is the first one whose rendering is nothing but the name.
//   * **`&T` is `Eq`, and that is what identity costs — nothing.** Address equality (`14 §2`) is
//     what "is this the lock you actually hold" and "did the heap take its own root off the end"
//     are written with. Worth recording as a positive, because a language without it forces a
//     unique name compared as a string, which is wrong in a way that only shows up once two objects
//     are named alike.
//
// Deliberately not modelled, so the traces stay computable on paper: more than one processor,
// preemption inside a tick, and inheritance through a *chain* of holders (a lends to b lends to c).
// The first two are not language questions; the third is the same decrease-key one more time.

// One thing a task does. `Work` is the only step that costs time — a lock taken and a lock dropped
// are decisions rather than labour — which is what makes the length of a run the sum of the work in
// it plus whatever the processor stood idle.
enum Step
    Work(ticks: int)
    Lock(m: &Mutex)
    Unlock(m: &Mutex)
    Sleep(ticks: int)
end Step

// Where a task is in its life. `Fresh` is before its arrival time, which is how a run is given a
// shape without a separate list of pending events.
enum State
    Fresh
    Ready
    Blocked
    Sleeping
    Done
    Faulted
end State

describe_state(s: State) -> string = s match
    Fresh -> "fresh"
    Ready -> "ready"
    Blocked -> "blocked"
    Sleeping -> "sleeping"
    Done -> "done"
    Faulted -> "faulted"

// What a task can get wrong. Both are about a lock, because a lock is the only thing in this
// machine that a task can hold an opinion about which the machine is in a position to check.
enum Fault
    NotHeld(lock: string)
    AlreadyHeld(lock: string)
end Fault

describe_fault(f: Fault) -> string = f match
    NotHeld(lock) -> s"released $lock without holding it"
    AlreadyHeld(lock) -> s"took $lock while already holding it"

// How a run ended. `Stalled` is the interesting one: every task that is not finished is blocked, so
// no future tick can differ from this one — a deadlock detected rather than waited out.
enum Outcome
    Running
    Finished(at: int)
    Stalled(at: int)
    Timeout(at: int)
end Outcome

describe_outcome(o: Outcome) -> string = o match
    Running -> "running"
    Finished(at) -> s"finished at $at"
    Stalled(at) -> s"stalled at $at"
    Timeout(at) -> s"timed out at $at"

// A task control block. `prio` is what the scheduler orders by and `base` is what the task was
// created with; the two differ exactly while the task is being lent somebody else's urgency, which
// is the whole of priority inheritance seen from the task's side.
struct Task
    name: string
    base: int
    prio: int
    arrive: int
    steps: []Step
    pc: usize
    did: int
    ran: int
    seq: int
    slot: Option[usize]
    state: State
    wake: int
    holds: &Buf[&Mutex]
    blocked_on: Option[&Mutex]
    fault: Option[Fault]

    // The letter this task writes into the trace. One character per task keeps a schedule short
    // enough to read, and short enough to check against the one worked out on paper.
    mark(self) -> u8 = self.name.bytes[0usize]

    key(self) -> Urgency = Urgency(self.prio, self.seq)

    is_fresh(self) -> bool = self.state match
        Fresh -> true
        _ -> false

    is_ready(self) -> bool = self.state match
        Ready -> true
        _ -> false

    settled(self) -> bool = self.state match
        Done -> true
        Faulted -> true
        _ -> false

    // Whether some future tick could still see this task run. A blocked task is not waiting for
    // time to pass, it is waiting for another task to act, so it does not count.
    movable(self) -> bool = self.state match
        Fresh -> true
        Ready -> true
        Sleeping -> true
        _ -> false
end Task

// A new task, with the five zeroes and the three `None`s every task starts life holding.
task(name: string, prio: int, arrive: int, steps: []Step) -> &Task
    Task(name, prio, prio, arrive, steps, 0usize, 0, 0, 0, None, Fresh, 0, buf(), None, None)
end task

// A lock. `owner` and the owner's `holds` are the same fact written from both ends, which is what
// lets a release know whose priority to hand back without going looking for it.
struct Mutex
    name: string
    owner: Option[&Task]
    waiters: &Buf[&Task]
end Mutex

mutex(name: string) -> &Mutex
    Mutex(name, None, buf())
end mutex

// Take one element out of a list that only grows and shrinks at its end: shift the survivors down,
// then drop the tail. `Buf` has neither a remove nor a truncate, so both halves are written out —
// and both are needed, since leaving the tail alone would leave the last element in twice.
drop_one[T: Eq](b: &Buf[T], v: T)
    var k = 0usize

    for i in 0..<b.len()
        if b[i] != v
            b.set(k, b[i])
            k += 1usize

    while b.len() > k
        b.pop()
end drop_one

// The waiter a lock should be handed to. A wait list is short and is walked rather than kept in
// order, which is the trade a kernel makes too: the cost is paid once per release, where keeping a
// second heap ordered would be paid on every block.
most_urgent(b: &Buf[&Task]) -> Option[&Task]
    if b.is_empty() then return None

    var best = b[0usize]

    for i in 1usize..<b.len()
        if before(b[i], best) then best = b[i]

    Some(best)
end most_urgent

// The machine. `inherit` is the one switch the program is built around: everything else about the
// two runs in the priority-inversion sections is identical.
struct Sched
    ready: RunQueue
    tasks: &Buf[&Task]
    sleeping: &Buf[&Task]
    now: int
    seq: int
    inherit: bool
    trace: &Buf[u8]
    idle: int

    add(*self, t: &Task) -> &Task
        self.tasks.push(t)
        t
    end add

    // Becoming ready stamps the task with the moment it did, which is what `before` breaks ties
    // with. Stamping it on *every* entry — including the re-entry of a task that has just spent a
    // tick — is what makes equal priorities share the processor a tick at a time rather than one of
    // them holding it until it blocks.
    make_ready(*self, t: &Task)
        t.state = Ready
        self.seq += 1
        t.seq = self.seq
        self.ready.push(t)
    end make_ready

    // What a task's priority should be right now: its own, unless it is holding a lock somebody
    // more urgent is waiting for, in which case it is theirs. Computing it from the graph rather
    // than remembering it is what keeps it right when a second waiter arrives, when a waiter is
    // handed the lock, and when the task lets go of one lock while still holding another.
    urgency(self, t: &Task) -> int
        var p = t.base

        for i in 0..<t.holds.len()
            var m = t.holds[i]

            for j in 0..<m.waiters.len()
                if m.waiters[j].prio < p then p = m.waiters[j].prio

        p
    end urgency

    // Give a task the priority the graph says it should have, and move it in the queue if it is in
    // one. This is the only writer of `prio`, which is what makes "a lent priority is always given
    // back" a property of one function rather than of every call site.
    reprice(*self, t: &Task)
        if !self.inherit then return

        var p = self.urgency(t)

        if p != t.prio
            t.prio = p
            self.ready.reprio(t)
    end reprice

    // Taking a lock. Free, and it is yours; held by somebody else, and you go on its waiter list
    // and lend it your urgency; held by you already, and that is a mistake the machine names.
    acquire(*self, t: &Task, m: &Mutex) -> bool
        var held = m.owner match
            Some(o) -> true
            None -> false

        if !held
            m.owner = Some(t)
            t.holds.push(m)
            return true

        var o = m.owner.unwrap()

        if o == t
            t.fault = Some(AlreadyHeld(m.name))
            t.state = Faulted
            return false

        t.state = Blocked
        t.blocked_on = Some(m)
        m.waiters.push(t)
        self.reprice(o)
        false
    end acquire

    // Letting go. The lock is handed straight to the most urgent waiter rather than thrown open for
    // whoever runs next — which is what makes a release deterministic, and what stops a task that
    // has been waiting from losing the lock to one that has not.
    //
    // The waiter's program counter moves past the `Lock` step it blocked on, because it did not
    // take the lock, it was given it: leaving the counter where it was would send it round to ask
    // for a lock it now owns, which the machine would rightly call a mistake.
    release(*self, t: &Task, m: &Mutex) -> bool
        var mine = m.owner match
            Some(o) -> o == t
            None -> false

        if !mine
            t.fault = Some(NotHeld(m.name))
            t.state = Faulted
            return false

        drop_one(t.holds, m)
        m.owner = None

        var next = most_urgent(m.waiters)

        if next.is_some()
            var w = next.unwrap()

            drop_one(m.waiters, w)
            w.blocked_on = None
            w.pc += 1usize
            m.owner = Some(w)
            w.holds.push(m)
            self.make_ready(w)
            self.reprice(w)

        self.reprice(t)
        true
    end release

    // Run the task's program until it is sitting on a step that costs time, or until it can go no
    // further. The answer is whether it can spend the tick it was picked for: a task that blocked,
    // slept, finished or made a mistake gives the tick back and somebody else is picked.
    advance(*self, t: &Task) -> bool
        loop
            if t.pc >= t.steps.len
                t.state = Done
                return false

            t.steps[t.pc] match
                Work(n) ->
                    if t.did < n then return true

                    t.did = 0
                    t.pc += 1usize
                Sleep(n) ->
                    t.pc += 1usize
                    t.state = Sleeping
                    t.wake = self.now + n
                    self.sleeping.push(t)
                    return false
                Lock(m) ->
                    if !self.acquire(t, m) then return false

                    t.pc += 1usize
                Unlock(m) ->
                    if !self.release(t, m) then return false

                    t.pc += 1usize
    end advance

    // Arrivals and wakeups, both of which are "the clock reached the number this task was waiting
    // for". Sleepers are compacted the same way a waiter list is, and for the same reason.
    admit(*self)
        for i in 0..<self.tasks.len()
            var t = self.tasks[i]

            if t.is_fresh() && t.arrive <= self.now then self.make_ready(t)

        var k = 0usize

        for i in 0..<self.sleeping.len()
            var t = self.sleeping[i]

            if t.wake <= self.now then self.make_ready(t)
            else
                self.sleeping.set(k, t)
                k += 1usize

        while self.sleeping.len() > k
            self.sleeping.pop()
    end admit

    // One tick of the machine: admit whoever the clock has reached, then hand the tick to the most
    // urgent ready task. A task that turns out not to be able to use it — it blocks on a lock, goes
    // to sleep, or has nothing left to do — is out of the queue by then, so the next pick is
    // somebody else, and the tick is lost only if nobody at all can take it.
    tick(*self)
        self.admit()

        var spent = false

        loop
            if spent then break

            self.ready.pop() match
                None -> break
                Some(t) ->
                    if self.advance(t)
                        self.trace.push(t.mark())
                        t.did += 1
                        t.ran += 1
                        spent = true

                        // The same walk again, which is what rolls the counter past a step whose
                        // last tick this was and runs whatever instantaneous steps follow it — so a
                        // lock is released in the tick the work finished, not in the next one.
                        self.advance(t)

                        if t.is_ready() then self.make_ready(t)

        if !spent
            self.idle += 1
            self.trace.push(46u8)

        self.now += 1
    end tick

    // Where the run stands. Nothing left alive is a finish; something alive but nothing able to
    // move is a deadlock, and it is worth reporting as its own answer rather than as a run that
    // used up its budget — a stall at tick 9 and a timeout at tick 400 are different bugs.
    status(self) -> Outcome
        var live = 0
        var movable = 0

        for i in 0..<self.tasks.len()
            var t = self.tasks[i]

            if !t.settled() then live += 1
            if t.movable() then movable += 1

        if live == 0 then Finished(self.now)
        elif movable == 0 then Stalled(self.now)
        else Running
    end status

    run(*self, limit: int) -> Outcome
        loop
            var s = self.status()
            var going = s match
                Running -> true
                _ -> false

            if !going then return s
            if self.now >= limit then return Timeout(self.now)

            self.tick()
    end run

    // The schedule, one letter per tick, with `.` for a tick nobody could use. This is the whole
    // assertion: two runs that differ anywhere differ here.
    schedule(self) -> string = from_utf8(self.trace.view()).unwrap_or("?")
end Sched

sched(inherit: bool) -> &Sched
    Sched(run_queue(), buf(), buf(), 0, 0, inherit, buf(), 0)
end sched
